{
  "annotator_prompt": [
    {
      "name": "held_color",
      "value_type": "text",
      "description": "After some closed transfers, the release-motion samples already show an empty open hand and do not reveal the released cube's color. The deposited counts alone therefore fail SUFFICIENCY and PREDICTABILITY. Add held_color, with values none, blue, green, or red, initially none. Record the color when a successful grasp is observed; retain it while the cube remains held, including at the closed transfer endpoint before release. Clear it at the first cycle showing the hand empty after release or loss. This field describes observed possession, not an intended grasp or an opening command. For example, write red at a closed transfer endpoint and none at the following completed-release cycle. Existing deposit-count annotations retain their completed-release timing."
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "name": "held_color",
      "description": "Sparse release samples can omit the previously held cube, making both its deposit update and the next action unpredictable from counts alone. Initialize held_color to none. Set it to the visibly grasped cube's color when the current views and finger opening establish a successful grasp. Preserve it through closed lifting and transfer, including temporary visual occlusion while possession remains supported. When the hand becomes empty, first use the incoming held_color to evaluate a new release over the bin, then write none. Clear it without counting if the observed loss occurred away from the bin. Do not set it from the cube selected for a future grasp or clear it merely because an opening command is about to be issued. Example: red."
    },
    {
      "name": "deposited_blue",
      "description": "When release samples start after separation, the existing requirement for a visible blue carried or falling cube can leave this count unchanged despite an annotated completed release, breaking PREDICTABILITY and sometimes SUFFICIENCY. Keep the existing initialization and evidence rules, but additionally increment once when incoming held_color is blue and the current cycle establishes an empty open hand over the bin following the completed release motion, with no evidence of loss elsewhere. Use incoming held_color before clearing it. Do not require the sampled frames to contain the cube itself. A no-step motion or a hand that was already empty does not qualify. Example: 1. Include only when blue is requested."
    },
    {
      "name": "deposited_green",
      "description": "Apply the same sparse-release correction consistently to green: the previous held color can be the only available evidence identifying a newly released cube. Keep existing initialization and release tests, and also increment once when incoming held_color is green and the current cycle establishes an empty open hand over the bin following the completed release motion, with no evidence of loss elsewhere. Evaluate this before clearing held_color. Never count a command about to execute, an already empty hand, or a no-step motion. Example: 2. Include only when green is requested."
    },
    {
      "name": "deposited_red",
      "description": "A red release can disappear before the first sampled incoming frame, causing the existing rule to preserve the old count even though the annotated count increases. This breaks PREDICTABILITY. Keep existing initialization and release tests, and additionally increment once when incoming held_color is red and the current cycle establishes an empty open hand over the bin following the completed release motion, with no evidence of loss elsewhere. Evaluate this before clearing held_color; the cube need not appear in the release samples. Never count a future command, an already empty hand, or a no-step motion. Example: 2. Include only when red is requested."
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "Sparse release samples sometimes left the hand empty over the bin without identifying the released cube. The policy then failed to update completion and could approach another cube instead of retreating. This is a SUFFICIENCY failure. Extend the effective-count calculation to use the revised memory release test, including incoming held_color, exactly once. An established transition from held cube to empty open hand over the bin requires the usual release retreat even when separation is absent from sampled frames. Compute completion from those effective counts, not from the cleared outgoing held_color.\n\nThe mandatory two-camera rim reconstruction repeatedly sent securely held, already lifted cubes upward for visibility recovery when the bin was plainly visible in the front camera but cropped out of the wrist camera. It also blocked release or retreat when the held cube obscured only part of the opening. This breaks SUFFICIENCY. Replace the requirement for cross-camera correspondences with a hierarchy of calibrated geometric methods: use reliable two-view correspondences when available, but accept a single-view reconstruction when the visible bin geometry constrains it. In the single-view case, identify actual table-contact base corners and corresponding vertical wall edges where visible. Intersect base-corner rays with z=0, then fit the corresponding upper corners along world-vertical lines to obtain the rim plane. Back-project inner-rim edge points into that plane and fit the rectangular opening. Use visible opposing edges or reconstruct obscured portions from the fitted rectangle; do not substitute the centroid of an exposed dark fragment. Cross-check the reconstruction against all available visible edges, including partial wrist edges. Do not assume a numerical rim height unsupported by geometry. A cropped wrist image or a partially hidden opening alone is not a reason to recover visibility. Reserve a purposeful viewing motion for genuinely underconstrained geometry.\n\nUse the resulting world opening center consistently for closed transfer and separate release at z=0.20, and for open retreat at [bin_x-0.10, bin_y, 0.20]. Several retreat errors had the correct operation and count but an inaccurate reconstructed center; check opposing edge midlines and reprojection rather than shifting the center toward a visible side wall. Preserve the existing grasp, separate-opening, nonzero-motion, final-retreat, and button-contact rules.",
  "refused": "The reports correctly identify that choosing any accessible eligible cube does not uniquely reproduce the recorded selections. However, their proposed preferences do not form a consistent rule across the supplied trajectories. Nearest-TCP selection would explain some choices but contradict others where a farther cube of the same eligible color was selected. Fixed left/right preferences and color priorities likewise vary across scenes. One report even calls a target blue although the subsequent grasp and release establish that it was red. No observable universal ranking was established, and adding future target identities to memory would break PREDICTABILITY rather than repair selection. Consequently, do not add per-scene target coordinates, arbitrary left/right priorities, or a universal nearest-cube rule; exact selection remains unresolved by this batch.\n\nThe requests to use cube top-face centers rather than whole colored silhouettes are already explicitly covered by the action instruction. The small initial approach and retreat errors that named no missing information do not justify new memory or scene-specific coordinate corrections. The request for a particular perpendicular cube-edge yaw also supplies no general observable convention selecting that branch; both edge alignments are already allowed, and the stated sufficiency test concerns position and gripper state. Do not introduce a yaw history field or hard-coded angle list.\n\nDo not add remembered bin coordinates merely to compensate for wrist cropping: the bin is recoverable from current scene geometry, so such a field has not met NECESSITY. Also do not invent a fixed numerical rim height from the suggested single-view fallback; use observable geometry as specified instead."
}

