      "modify_from": "Reconstruct the bin's rectangular inner opening from calibrated current front and wrist views. Identify corresponding inner-rim corners or edge intersections. For each image point [u,v,1], form a world ray with camera center C=-R^T t and direction d=R^T K^-1[u,v,1], using that camera's current calibration. Triangulate reliable corresponding rim points and fit their common horizontal plane. Fit the rectangular inner opening to visible opposing edges in that plane; reconstruct occluded corners from the fitted edges rather than using the centroid of the visible dark fragment. Compute the world center from opposing corners or edge midlines, and check that the fitted opening reprojects onto visible rim segments in both views. Do not average image centroids across cameras, use occluding silhouette boundaries as rim correspondences, or intersect the opening center with the tabletop. If reliable reconstruction is unavailable, use the purposeful visibility-recovery motion below rather than inventing a rim height.",
      "modify_to": "Reconstruct the bin's rectangular inner opening using the available calibrated current views. For each image point [u,v,1], form a world ray with camera center C=-R^T t and direction d=R^T K^-1[u,v,1], using that camera's current calibration. Prefer reliable two-view correspondences between actual inner-rim corners or edge intersections: triangulate them and fit their common horizontal plane. When such correspondences are unavailable, accept a single-view reconstruction if visible geometry constrains it. Identify actual table-contact base corners and their corresponding vertical wall edges; intersect base-corner rays with z=0, then fit corresponding upper corners along the world-vertical lines through those base corners to determine the rim plane. Back-project inner-rim edge points into that plane and fit the rectangular opening. Use visible opposing edges and reconstruct obscured portions only where the fitted geometry constrains them. Compute the world center from opposing corners or edge midlines, and verify reprojection against all available visible rim segments, including partial wrist edges. Do not shift the center toward a visible side wall, use the centroid of an exposed dark fragment, average image centroids across cameras, treat occluding silhouettes as rim correspondences, or intersect the opening center with the tabletop. A cropped wrist view or partial opening occlusion alone does not require visibility recovery. Use the purposeful visibility-recovery motion below only when the available geometry is genuinely underconstrained; do not invent a numeric rim height."
    },
